  <section class="section" id="key-findings">
    <div class="section-header">
      <span class="section-num">06</span>
      <div>
        <div class="section-title">Key Findings and Strategic Takeaways</div>
        <div class="section-desc">Synthesized insights for players, coaches, and platform analysts.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p style="color:var(--text-muted); margin-bottom:28px;">
      The following findings represent the most actionable, non-obvious conclusions from the full analysis. Each is directly supported by the data and carries implications for how players, coaches, or platform designers should think about chess on Lichess.
    </p>

    <div class="finding">
      <div class="finding-num">1</div>
      <div class="finding-body">
        <strong>White's first-move advantage is real but modest — and shrinks with skill.</strong>
        <p>White wins 52.3% of decisive games overall, a 4.7 pp edge over black. But at the 2000+ bracket, this narrows to just 50.6%, barely above a coin flip among decisive games. Coaches working with stronger players should emphasize that color matters less at high levels — preparation quality and middlegame execution dominate.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="finding">
      <div class="finding-num green" style="background:var(--accent3);">2</div>
      <div class="finding-body">
        <strong>A 100-point Elo gap is the threshold where rating becomes reliably predictive.</strong>
        <p>Below 50-point gaps, the higher-rated player wins only ~49% — effectively random. Above 100 points, win probability climbs to 62.4% and above; above 200 points, to 75.3%. Rating systems work, but only when the differential is meaningful. Platform matchmaking that targets &lt;50-point pairings creates genuinely competitive games where outcome is highly uncertain.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="finding">
      <div class="finding-num red" style="background:var(--accent2);">3</div>
      <div class="finding-body">
        <strong>Van't Kruijs (1.e3) is the worst-performing first move in this dataset — by far.</strong>
        <p>White wins only 34.2% of games with Van't Kruijs — well below any other top-15 opening. Black wins 61.4%. This opening is disproportionately chosen by lower-rated players who may not realize how passive it is. A simple intervention: Lichess's opening explorer could flag openings with dramatically unfavorable empirical win rates at specific skill levels.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="finding">
      <div class="finding-num yellow" style="background:var(--accent4);">4</div>
      <div class="finding-body">
        <strong>Resignation dominates — most games don't reach checkmate or timeout.</strong>
        <p>55.6% of games end in resignation, 31.5% in checkmate, just 8.4% in timeout. Resign rates are consistent across time controls, suggesting that players at this skill level (~1400–1800 average rating) are generally competent at recognizing losing positions. The checkmate rate of 31.5% is notably higher than at the grandmaster level, where resignation comes much earlier.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="finding">
      <div class="finding-num">5</div>
      <div class="finding-body">
        <strong>Clock increments dramatically reduce timeout losses — increments are not cosmetic.</strong>
        <p>The 8+0 time control produces a 17.9% timeout rate — nearly 3× the dataset average. Adding even small increments (15+15 drops this to 3.6%) fundamentally changes the game's outcome distribution. For platforms designing default time controls, increments serve a measurable quality-of-game function, not just a preference feature.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="finding">
      <div class="finding-num green" style="background:var(--accent3);">6</div>
      <div class="finding-body">
        <strong>Higher-rated players play longer games — a diagnostic signal for skill.</strong>
        <p>Average game length increases monotonically from 50.0 turns at &lt;1200 to 68.1 turns at 2000+. Higher-rated players resist early collapses, sustain complex middlegames, and convert endgames more efficiently rather than flagging quickly. This turn-count correlation (skill → game length) is a platform-observable signal that could supplement Elo in experience and skill assessment.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="finding">
      <div class="finding-num red" style="background:var(--accent2);">7</div>
      <div class="finding-body">
        <strong>Black's timeout advantage is the one place white's edge disappears.</strong>
        <p>In out-of-time games, white wins only 49.7% — statistical parity. This counterintuitive result may reflect that white, as the attacker, often plays more complex/committal positions that are harder to manage under time pressure, or that black's more reactive play is naturally faster to execute. For time-pressured players: simplify and trade pieces when low on clock, regardless of color.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

